B. Markov Decision Process with Perturbation A deep reinforcement learning task can be considered as
a continuous decision-making problem, which follows the Markov Decision Process (MDP) [25]. Basic MDP is defined as (S, A, P, R, γ). The agent interacts with the environment of successive state s ∈ S with actions a ∈ A, getting the step reward r ∈ R and the transition probability p ∈ P to the next
′
state s ∈ S. γ indicates the discount factor. The goal of deep reinforcement learning is to find an optimal policy that has the maximum accumulative discounted returns. In this work,

to ensure the robust policy against perturbation, we define the Markov Decision Process with observation adversary (OAMDP).
In contrast to MDP, OA-MDP consists of a tuple of (S, A, P, R, γ, SA, AA), where sA ∈ SA is the set of states from S under optimal adversarial attack, and aA ∈ AA is the action the agent responded to sA. The attacked state sA is a shifted state, which models the worst case perturbation due to attacks on the sensor leading to aA sub-optimal than a. A welltrained guidance policy network may be able to cope with a weak and quick perturbation, turning back to the actual actions and the desired trajectory after the state observations get back to normal. However under strong and continuous adversarial attacks, this guidance policy network can easily fail.

