The distance f rom center refers to distance between the center of the ego vehicle and the road center, and the angle denotes the angle between the car and the vector of two neighboring way-points. Both of the variables are in range of [0, 1]. We set rcompound = V ∗ centering ∗ angling, and V changes depending on the vehicle speed. When the speed is between 12 km/h and 22 km/h, V = 1. If the speed is below 12, V = velocity/12 and if the speed exceeds 22 km/h, V = 1 − (velocity − 22)/3. This setting aims to encourage the DRL agent to maintain a speed within the range of 12 km/h to 22 km/h. In summary, the reward function balances the encouragement for goal reaching, obstacle avoidance and safety. Furthermore, our adversarial attack defence algorithm is independent from the reward function. Both the baseline and our approach are trained using the same reward function, meaning that the efficiency of our method is hardly affected by the design of the reward function.

G. Adversarial Attack Detection Network based Explainability
In this section, we introduce an efficient explainability based adversarial attack detection network for attack awareness and better understanding the fundamental of the decision making process under adversarial attacks. We adopt saliency map to provide insights into the DRL model’s decision-making process by highlighting the most influential regions in the input space. Saliency maps help understand which parts of the input contribute most to the agent’s action. With an analysis made we found out that the saliency maps generated from the same policy show certain discrepancies under normal states and adversarial attacked states. This spurred our decision to propose a deep network-based detector (CNN-based detector) exploiting explainability based saliency maps by monitoring the saliency maps generated by the gradient of the robust proximal policy optimization agent. The detector will learn and find patterns in the saliency map images to detect adversarial attacks.
By calculating the gradient of the output with respect to the feature map from the last convolutional layer, the saliency maps are obtained. The map is represented as a heatmap, and it will be up-sampled to overlay on the sensor RGB image input to form the final visualization. We collect our own adversarial attack detection data in Carla using the trained agent driving around. The details of the attack detection dataset will be explained in the experiment section.
We design an optimized convolutional neural network architecture to balance the detection accuracy and the computation

burden. It consists of three convolutional layers with channel numbers of 16, 32, and 64, respectively. All convolutional layers have a kernel size of 3, stride of 1, and padding of 1. Three linear layers are then connected to the last convolutional layer with output neurons of 128, 64, and 2 for the final prediction of adversarial or normal. Batch normalization layers are employed after the second convolutional layer and the second linear layer to stabilize the training process. ReLU activation functions are used, and the network is initialized using Kaiming uniform initialization. The details of the network is shown in Fig. 5. Crossentropy loss function is used to fit the network outputs and the supervised signals.
